I generally try to get all of my armor enchanted to
at least +3, preferably higher, in the middle game.

> > especially with that shirt, which does
> > nothing on its own,

Although it's true that a +0 shirt does nothing
but weigh you down (a little), it wouldn't always
necessarily be my first choice to enchant.  If
I have other armor that I intend to keep through
the rest of the game, I would usually enchant it
first, at least until it's +2.  (Once you hit +2, it's
arguable that you would want to use a blessed
scroll on something that's +0 instead, because
an uncursed scroll can bring the +2 armor up
to +3, and then it can still be enchanted once
more later.)

All else being equal, first priority should be
given to armor that does something more
with its enchantment than just AC, e.g., a
helm of brilliance or gauntlets of dexterity.
After that, enchant any other armor that
you intend to keep to the end of the game.
Get it all up to +2 with blessed scrolls
(or +3 if it goes there) then when you have
more scrolls after that you can enchant
some of it higher.

Rustproofing should be done with scrolls
of destroy armor if you have them, because
they have no other use.  (Even if you're using
a marker, they're cheaper to write than
enchant armor, though I'm not sure I'd use
a marker to write scrolls for rustproofing in
most cases; I'd probably just wait until I can
collect more scrolls later.  There might be
exceptions, though, e.g., if you're going to
be around lava and have water walking boots
it might be worth using marker charges on
a scroll to fireproof them.  In such cases,
using the scroll of destroy armor is more
efficient than the scroll of enchant armor.)

> > If you have a wish to wrest, use it on a magic marker.

I believe I agree with this in the general case.

Of course, there could be situations where there would
be a greater need, e.g., you don't have magic resistance
and really need to wish for that.

> > The only reason to not use your wishes in your wand
> > is if you're saving them for an emergency--and you
> > can't really depend on wresting in an emergency.

There is another reason to save wishes, sometimes:  if
you don't know what you're going to need yet, because
you aren't sure what you're going to get, there may be
times when it is worth waiting to see.

This is of course not always the case.  If you're wearing
junk body armor, for instance, you generally want to go
ahead and get DSM as soon as possible, NOT wait to
see what dragons you run across later and which colors
drop scales in case you can get DSM without wishing for
it.  Having it now will keep you alive.

But in other cases, there are things you can wait to see.
If you happen across an early wand (or smoky potions),
it may be worthwhile to complete Sokoban first, especially
if you're almost there, so you know whether you're going
to have an early source of reflection or not.  (Depending
on other factors, this might make the difference between
wishing for silver versus gray DSM.)  If you've recently
picked up an identify spell and haven't yet gone back
and consolidated your caches and identified all the
stuff you've picked up, you probably want to do that
before wishing for something you MAY already have.
And so on.

Note, however, that there are all _temporary_ situations.

